  "query_preview": "..\n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThe upper-level ridge pattern continues Friday which will bring \nanother day of hot temperatures to the region. Drier PWATs and \nlittle forcing Friday will keep most of the region dry, but I can't \nrule out a few showers and thunderstorms in Southest MT during the\nafternoon. Temperatures peak on Saturday as the ridge breakdowns \nand southwest flow increases. Lower elevations are forecasted to \nreach into the 100s across north-Central MT and the upper 90s in \nSouthwest MT. The ridge breakdown also brings an increase in 700mb\nwinds through Sunday, peaking late Saturday night into early \nSunday morning to 60-70kts along the Northern Rocky Mountain \nFront. As a result, strong winds develop along the Rocky Moutnain \nFront windy conditions develop elsewhere Saturday and Sunday. This\nwith the combination of hot temperatures and low relative \nhumdiity creates concerns for elevated to critical fire weather \nconditions (see FIRE WEATHER section). \n\nA mostly dry frontal passage Sunday will help bring a relief in \nthe heat through the first half of next week. Northwest fllow \naloft will help keep temepratures down throguh Tuesday, before \nvery warm temperatures return. Low moisutre throughout the week \nwill keep conditions dry.\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nHeat this Weekend:\n\nThere remains good confidence in imapctful heat setting up Saturday \nacross the region, especially North-Central MT. Based on recent \ntemeprautre trends this week wiht the wildfire smoke, the NBM may be \nrunning up to few degrees too hot for Saturday. However, there is \ngood confidence for widespread temepratures in the 100s across North-\nCentral MT Saturday. Therefore, the current heat products remain on \ntrack.\n\nWinds Saturday and Sunday:\n\nModels have been consitstent on the increase in winds and the timing \nof peak winds. There is a 30-60% chance for 60 mph winds along the \nimmediate eastern slopes of the Rocky Mountain Front. Although this \nis a typically windy region, it will be unusually windy for the time \nof year, as wind gusts exceed the 95th percentile for August. \nTherefore, these wind speeds are more imapctful for the time of \nyear. The main imapct will be for outdoor recrecreation, \nparticualry for Glacier NP and those traveling in high profile \nvehicles. -Wilson",
